    "md5": "ec2eea7fb99c4bc77c9c261df7cdc87d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1733,
    "chunk_text": "An intellectual discourse is needed on religious issues\n\nThe United National Party (UNP) has criticised the government for its move to establish a special court or a Dharmadhikaranaya to resolve issues among the Buddhist clergy, claiming that government cannot establish such a court.\n\nTrue, but it seems to be a delusion on the part of the UNP if not for an attempt to mislead the country, since President Anura Kumara Dissanayake, during his first announcement on the matter,\u00a0 said that he accepts the suggestion put forward by the Maha Sangha in respect of a Dharmadhikaranaya to resolve issues being cropped up among monks,\u00a0 including ones pertaining to discipline.\n\nThe suggestion has come from at least a section of the Buddhist monks apparently subsequent to the recent allegations of misbehaviour of certain monks. In fact, the government was also widely accused in social media of dragging its feet in taking action against Atamasthanadhipathi Ven. Pallegama Hemarathana Thera, a leading monk who is out on bail and removed from his high-ranking position after he was accused of sexual abuse.\n\nOne solace for the government in this case is that the Opposition, which usually makes a huge fuss at the drop of a hat, has been notably silent on these recent incidents. No party in Opposition has been expressing their views on these incidents or alleged incidents. Nor have they shown any interest in discussing the allegations against the National Child Protection Authority (NCPA) and the police over their lethargic attitude towards these incidents,\u00a0 which are a main issue debated in the social media. This indifference or\u00a0 fear at the political level indicates the level of sensitivity the incidents in question carry."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-06-01-dailymirrorlk-6e129e30",
