    "md5": "1ea5b6d7e854907e8e8b8a8a46f70902",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2024,
    "chunk_text": "Work on a vaccine for latest Ebola variant is in progress\n\nA healthcare worker sprays a room during the funeral of a person suspected of dying of Ebola in\u00a0 the Democratic Republic of Congo\n\nCalled the \u201cBundibugyo Ebolavirus\u201d, the latest strain of Ebola is very contagious, but not very much of a killer\n\nA new type of Ebola called the \u201cBundibugyo ebolavirus\u201d is spreading rapidly in Central Africa, worrying international health organisations as there is no vaccine for it yet. Work on a vaccine is in progress in the UK, US and India, but it will be a while before the vaccine can be used on humans.\u00a0 Since the first Ebola outbreak in 1976 in what\u2019s now the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC) and South Sudan, the virus has killed over 15,000 people across this\u00a0 part of Central Africa. In the latest episode, about\u00a0 1000 people may have died, according to estimates.\n\nCalled the \u201cBundibugyo Ebolavirus\u201d, the latest strain of Ebola is very contagious, but not very much of a killer. The latest outbreak occurred in the Ituri province in northeastern Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC), a war-torn area that borders Uganda and South Sudan.\n\nThe first known patient, a health worker, had developed symptoms including fever and vomiting on April 24 and died. According to the World Health Organisation (WHO), the virus spread further after that individual\u2019s virus-ridden body was transported home for burial.\n\nIn the last count, there are at least 930 suspected cases and 349 deaths in the DRC, and seven cases with one death in Uganda. This region is a major commercial hub and mining centre and is densely populated. Two million people had been displaced and 10 million face acute hunger here. Armed conflict has intensified over the past two months, making it difficult to get health assistance to the area.\n\nOn May 17, the WHO declared the current Ebola epidemic a \u201cPublic Health Emergency of International Concern\u201d, and days later, warned that it could be months before the outbreak was contained."
  },
