    "description": "Cybercrime  Safe Havens  in Colombo:  A National Security Failure",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/news-features/Cybercrime-Safe-Havens-in-Colombo-A-National-Security-Failure/131-341734",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1780335416.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-06-01-dailymirrorlk-99ccdcb6-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "52265e6c389a9ca8aca89c22ee504a8b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1731,
    "chunk_text": "Foreign nationals do not simply appear inside luxury apartments overnight. They arrive through airports, obtain visas, extend permits, lease office spaces, establish communications infrastructure, move funds and begin operations. Every one of these stages should leave a regulatory footprint.\n\nWas this due to incompetence, negligence or systemic failure by design?\n\nApartment owners are already complying with the law to the extent practically possible. Most landlords request copies of passports, visa documentation and work permits before renting out their premises. Beyond that, they possess neither the legal authority nor the technical capability to investigate or monitor activities taking place privately within apartments or offices.\n\nSri Lanka cannot expect ordinary civilians to perform the functions of intelligence officers, cyber investigators or immigration surveillance units. The danger arises when authorities attempt to transfer accountability downward because institutional systems higher up have failed.\n\nThe real issue lies in the absence of coordinated monitoring between immigration authorities, financial intelligence units, cybercrime investigators, telecommunications regulators and law enforcement agencies.\n\nCybercrime today is no longer confined to isolated online fraudsters operating from laptops. Many of these groups function as highly organised transnational criminal cartels with international linkages, encrypted communications, financial laundering systems and sophisticated operational structures. Some possess direct or indirect connections to organised crime syndicates involved in online gambling, narcotics trafficking, money laundering, identity theft and digital financial manipulation."
  },
