    "language": "en",
    "md5": "0eb965ac25a64321c90cb312149f22a6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1750,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cUnlike traditional hacking, social engineering bypasses firewalls and antivirus systems because the victim unknowingly opens the door themselves. This psychological dimension represents one of the most dangerous developments in the digital age.\u201d\n\nModern smartphones are prime targets because they store financial apps, biometrics, OTPs, personal communications, and authentication systems in a single device. A smartphone today is not merely a communication tool \u2014 it is effectively a portable digital identity vault.\n\nRisks include malicious applications, fake app downloads from unofficial stores, screen-sharing scams where a fraudster asks to view your phone, excessive app permissions (why does a flashlight app need your contacts?), public Wi-Fi interception, Bluetooth exploitation, and spyware. Subtle warning signs that may indicate malicious background activity include unexplained overheating, abnormal battery drain, unexpected data usage spikes, or strange pop-ups.\n\nSocial media has evolved into a massive intelligence collection platform. Every post potentially reveals location, daily routine, relationships, financial status, political opinions, emotional condition, travel habits, and personal interests. Cybercriminals use this information to build psychological profiles, launch targeted scams, conduct impersonation attacks, and manipulate trust networks. Your digital footprint is effectively a behavioral map of you.\n\nA digital footprint is the collection of data created through an individual\u2019s online activity. This includes search history, social media activity, location tracking, financial transactions, emails, voice recordings, smart device usage, website visits, biometric authentication, and online purchases."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-06-01-dailyftlk-f23a8e87",
